WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.160
How can you improve your listening?

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.960
Here's a question from Fatosh.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.040
My name is Fatosh.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.080
I'm from Turkey.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.520
I'm very busy.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.360
I don't have much time.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.160
How can I improve my listening?

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.360
That was Fatosh's question.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.840
But we also want your questions.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.440
You can send us an email at

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.040
learningenglish@bbc.co.uk

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.920
Now, back to listening. Let's go!

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.840
So, let's look at four tips
to improve your listening.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.320
First, if you don't have much time
then it's good to find something

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.520
that you're interested in.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.120
And actually most of these things you
can probably listen to on your phone.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.680
So you can do it almost anywhere.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.920
You can watch TV or films in English,
whether that's on TV or on your phone.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.120
Podcasts are another great way
to find English to listen to.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.640
Find one about a topic you like.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.280
Next, let's talk about subtitles.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.920
They can be helpful, but
turning them off can also be helpful.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.600
Subtitles in your language
can help you understand something

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.920
that's a bit difficult.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.960
Subtitles in English
can help you understand the sound

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.920
that different words or
different groups of words can make,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.120
and it can be more complicated
than you think.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.800
Listening without subtitles helps you
really practice listening on its own.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.800
Next tip. Before you listen
to something, try to predict

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.360
what things you might hear.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.040
Make a checklist.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.400
Then listen and tick off the things you hear.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.600
You could also make a list of questions

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.720
that you think will be answered in the next section.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.880
Then listen and see if you get the answers.
 

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.760
This gives you practice
at listening for specific information

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.640
and getting clues from what you hear.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.000
Next tip! The way that
English sounds can be surprising.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.160
There can be words that you don't hear,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.280
or words that don't sound the way
that you expect them to.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.280
Listen to something short and
try to write down the words you hear.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.560
Listen as many times as you need
and then check with the subtitles.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:31.280
Pay attention to words you've missed
or anything that sounds surprising.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.600
The most important thing is to find
things that you like listening to

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.240
and can understand most of.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.000
Then see if some
of these techniques can help.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.400
That's all for now, but you can
find out more on this topic below.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.880
See you next time!

